{"author":{"id":"40e3ba2788b3b9fc163c354afc44e5f9d225bcf0a27e679aaa878dd6135c6dcb"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794661970,"nonce":"3B26S2wYK/0WtG5SfFL674XlXNQ=","title":"Plan snapshot growth beyond current caps and read-only access","message":"Follow-up to a3bcfb4 after its scale and recovery validation. Alpha12 retains the existing v1/v2 format and exclusive writable lock.\n\nMeasurements: two complete 3.25 GiB logical workflows (1 GiB unique plus 2.25 GiB repeated), three independent restores each, a file crossing 2 GiB, and actual 100,000-entry/500,000-reference/64 MiB loose and packed manifest limits. A 4,023-byte repeated chunk exhausts the reference limit at 2,011,500,000 bytes, so the nominal 1 TiB byte ceiling is not a demonstrated collection capacity. SIGKILL and private-filesystem ENOSPC recovery passed; no hardware power-loss claim.\n\nNext design work:\n- Compare documented repository partitioning with an explicitly versioned larger packed format; measure representative reference density and manifest parsing/verification memory before selecting the approach.\n- Preserve v1/v2 readability, verified publication and migration recovery; avoid merely raising caps while retaining proportional in-memory metadata.\n- Assess read-only repositories and concurrent readers separately from the exclusive writer sentinel. Define authority during migration, SQLite read transactions, prune/repair interaction and stale-lock ownership before implementation.\n- Include the existing 2-million-chunk inventory and 100,000-history caps in the design; neither boundary was exercised by this validation.\n\nEvidence: docs/benchmarks/2026-11-14-scale-recovery.md and its JSON receipts. This issue records remaining design work, not a completed format expansion or new concurrency guarantee.","files":null}]}